### They want a gift the child will enjoy or keep, even when they already have plenty.

19 of 123 posts.

Most requests concern first or second birthdays and Christmas: what gets used, what suits the next stage, and what is left after siblings' hand-me-downs. They range from £100–150 for two occasions to a bath-toy set with no price concern, and include personalised keepsakes, outings, a voucher and a handmade play-food present. Some want an unusual toy purely for fun; others want fewer lasting gifts or memories of the occasion.

- "My little guy is about to turn one. He is my second baby and the youngest in two big families, so he has great hand-me-downs of practically every toy imaginable. I still would like to get him something fun for his 1st birthday and I’m completely stumped!" [sr114-0037]
- "I’m seeing lots of videos on instagram and tiktok and i’m just wondering what kids this age actually use and play with. Our budget is £100-150 for both events." [sr080-0109]
- "Any suggestions for a 2nd bday gift? Last year we got our son the little people barn set and he loves it. He’s at a weird stage where most of his toys are too young for him now, but I’m not sure what’s appropriate at this stage." [sr105-0112]
- "Sorry I asked about a gift for a 1 year old before and you all were so helpful!! Now looking for recommendations for a personalised gift that he can keep forever x" [sr106-0033]
- "Putting the pictures in the comments. I crocheted my son a ton of play food and got him a play grocery store for it." [sr070-0006]
- "I like the look of Lovevery’s bath toy kit, but would love to hear your favorites! Price isn’t a concern." [sr133-0024]

**But:** Buying another toy is not the only desired result; one first-Christmas post considers keeping the money: "Saving the money as they’re too little to understand?" [sr131-0070]

