### They want music equipment the child can enjoy and use, and question its value.

4 of 123 posts.

The children range from six to fifteen months, and the choices include Toniebox, Yoto and a cassette player. Parents ask for song-heavy content, cause and effect, independent operation, durability and value over time. One already bought a box and characters; another would record their own songs and dislikes the available generic music.

- "He’s really only interested in the songs! What tonies are song heavy / fun for toddlers? + any other recommendations?" [sr044-0037]
- "I have only 1 child, a 6 month old girl. Tonie boxes seem to be all the rage, we don’t have one. Is it worth it? I have no problem entertaining baby but I wonder if it would be a nice developmental / cause and effect type toy to have." [sr099-0113]
- "I've been looking into getting a yoto or toniebox but can't justify the price for a music player. I understand wanting to give the child independence, but she'll be 12 months old in Oct so she won't be swapping out characters or cards anytime soon anyway. (Yoto is looking better than Toniebox for us if I were to get one of the two)" [sr102-0009]
- "I've thought about purchasing a cassette player and just recording my own songs onto blank tapes. I'm not a huge fan of nursery rhymes for my baby and especially hate the generic, AI-sounding songs on the yoto and toniebox, so I'd be using the MYO cards or characters most of the time anyway." [sr102-0010]
- "But also, my boy is a literal mischief ape and will, in time, smash anything and everything we give him with an impish grin on his face. So I'm not sure we need to look too hard for something that will survive his entire childhood, because I don't really think anything will." [sr117-0069]

**But:** The six-month-old's parent does not describe needing help to entertain the child: "I have no problem entertaining baby but I wonder if it would be a nice developmental / cause and effect type toy to have." [sr099-0113]

