One mother on bed rest with osteitis pubis says she cannot hold her four-month-old and needs play that does not require her to move. Another parent is about to give birth and wants immediately available toys that keep a daring twenty-month-old occupied for family babysitters. Existing swings, soft toys, activity toys or climbing equipment have not solved the particular need, and the parent preparing for labour rules out multiple ingestion hazards.

- "i am a mother who is diagnosed with Osteitis Pubis caused due to pregnancy.i am on the bed rest currently and i am not allowed to even hold my baby.my husband has a full time job and he can't tend to him all the time.so i need something that would keep my baby engaged for a while without requiring me to be mobile." [sr129-0072]
- "All that said, I’m looking for toy ideas that I can buy right away that will keep her interest for a good chunk of the day or two I’m gone to help lower mine (and my family’s) stress." [sr112-0082]
- "Since I won’t be the one personally supervising and want to keep my worry level at the hospital as low as possible, anything with any type of choking/ingestion/other hazards are are off limits:" [sr112-0083]
- "he has few soft toys which he has no interest in and has electric swing and bouncer which he doesn't like 🥺.what should i buy? Battery operated toys like cars or what?" [sr129-0072]

