8 of 123 posts.

Requests range from a pulling-to-stand eight-and-a-half-month-old and a climbing ten-month-old to backyard equipment for toddlers and an older sibling. Parents compare walkers, climbing blocks, a play couch, a triangle, convertible trikes, pedal cars and swing sets. They want sustained use, fit with present ability, less bending to support walking, and confidence before spending hundreds or thousands.

- "Also if walkers are good to use, when? Should I wait until he's cruising? He isn't quite steady with pull to stand yet. But he is getting there." [sr107-0098]
- "My almost 10 month old is showing a lot of interest in trying to climb recently. I’m debating between a pikler triangle, nugget couch, or foam climbing blocks. I feel like the blocks might be best for her age, but I was planning on a nugget eventually and it seems like overkill to have both. I like the pikler but it seems like that might be better when she’s a little bigger? Will probably get one of these for her birthday in October or for Christmas. Thoughts?" [sr094-0018]
- "What is your highest recommendation to keep your toddler occupied in the backyard (from simple objects to more complex toys). Anything they’ve used way more than you thought they would or that they ignored after 1-2 uses (so it wasn’t worth either money or set up… or both)? We have a few very active 16 month old boy. Thanks in advance for any recommendations!" [sr016-0104]
- "We're ok with getting something that's recommended for 3+ as we want to use this for years to come. We would like something with a swing and slide. I'm hesitant to get a wooden set as I know they can require extra maintenance, but open to it if it has excellent reviews and assembly options (we are not handy people)." [sr021-0038]
- "But I'm obviously nervous spending $3-4K on something I can't see or touch first." [sr093-0104]
- "I ordered a baby walker (even if I know that's not the best option, I don't expect my baby to use it for hours)." [sr023-0013]

