### They want shoes that fit small or developing feet and work for the child's actual walking and weather.

8 of 123 posts.

These posts include an early walker who will be nine months, eleven- and twelve-month-old walkers, fifteen-month-olds needing nursery boots, and an active twenty-two-month-old. Parents weigh grip, flexibility, width, sensory feedback, weather protection and price; small sizes may have fabric soles while rubber-soled sizes are too large. One newly one-year-old does not walk yet but daycare requires shoes, and the parent accidentally ordered adult men's shoes.

- " I want some that have nothing to do with looks, all to do with foot development, comfort, good grip for playing, and affordability. I’m kind of tired of this era of endless options for baby’s and kids because it’s all money grab nonsense. I just want to know what works well and that’s it lol so pls help a mama out. Thank you!" [sr031-0012]
- "But all the ones I’ve found with actual rubber bottoms are size 4 and huge on her. I know eventually she’ll grow into them but right now they’re just a tripping hazard." [sr083-0089]
- "She doesn't walk independently but can while holding someone's hand and can cruise around furniture, etc. fine. I got her measured at clarks, and she's a size 4g. She does have some pre walkers, but they don't appear to be super water resistant." [sr096-0087]
- "Trying to determine the best form of shoe to get our 11 month old baby for beginning to walk. I know that there are some discussions between flat feet prevention (something that both baby's mother and I slightly suffer from) and ensuring sensory feedback with the ground. I want to make sure that baby is building a connection and spatial awareness with the ground." [sr122-0045]
- "They arrived on my doorstep. It was adult men’s shoes. LOL now I have to go to Walmart to return them." [sr073-0026]

**But:** The need for shoes does not always come from independent walking: "My boy just turned 1 and can’t walk yet, but he is moving up to the toddler room at his daycare and they require all the toddlers wear shoes." [sr073-0024]

